// ======== Meshing: surface nets, on the CPU ========
//
// A shape made into triangles for drawing: the field sampled on a grid of
// cubic cells, one vertex in each cell the surface crosses, and a quad
// across each grid edge whose ends lie on opposite sides of it, joining the
// four cells round that edge (Gibson's surface nets, 1998, as Lysenko's
// "naive surface nets" writes it up, 0fps.net, 2012). A cell's vertex is the
// average of the points where the surface crosses its edges (each found by
// linear interpolation of the two ends' distances), optionally moved onto
// the surface along the gradient, and its normal is the field's gradient
// there (central differences), not a face's.
//
// ONE VERTEX PER PIECE, SO THE MESH IS A MANIFOLD. Where the surface passes
// through a cell twice (two thin parts close together, or a gap narrower
// than a cell), naive surface nets gives the cell one vertex and the mesh a
// pinch: an edge four quads share, or a vertex two cones of quads meet at.
// Here a cell gets one vertex for each separate piece of surface crossing
// it, the pieces marching cubes would draw there (Nielson's dual marching
// cubes, 2004, by the same idea): on each face of the cell the surface's
// crossings are paired as the inside corners' runs round the face separate
// them (on a face with two inside corners diagonally opposite, each inside
// corner is cut off on its own), and the crossings a chain of such pairs
// joins are one piece. Neighbouring cells pair the crossings on the face
// they share the same way, so every quad edge is shared by exactly two
// quads, and a closed shape inside the grid gives a closed, manifold mesh.
// The one case left, two pieces of one face joined round both cells, needs
// a feature thinner than a cell on both sides of a face; PolyMesh's
// 'validate' reports it. A tube thinner than a cell (the horn's tip) keeps
// its topology but can fold a quad or two to face against the gradient. The pieces for each of the 256 ways a cell's
// corners fall are worked out once per mesh, in 'netTables'.
//
// SPARSE. The grid is tested in blocks of NetBlock cells a side: the
// distance at a block's centre, if it is more than half the block's
// diagonal (with a margin of 1%), means no point of the block is on the
// surface when the field is 1-Lipschitz (exact distances and bounds are),
// so every sample in it has the centre's sign and none is evaluated. A
// sample is evaluated only if some block holding it may meet the surface;
// every edge the surface crosses has both ends evaluated, so the mesh is
// bit for bit the one every sample would give (tests/meshing.cone checks).
//
// DETERMINISTIC. Everything is visited in a fixed order (z, then y, then x;
// the pieces of a cell by their first edge), sums are made in that order,
// and nothing depends on addresses or timing: the same shape and numbers
// give the same bits every run and in debug and release builds.
// mesh's 'meshHash' is a hash of those bits, for pinning them.
//
// Surface nets rounds sharp edges and corners off (a vertex is an average,
// not where the faces' planes meet): dual contouring, which keeps them, is
// not built.

// Cells a side of a block the sparse pass tests at once
const NetBlock = 4usize;

// A piece number meaning "the surface does not cross this edge"
const NoPiece = 255u8;

